>[]($block.attrs("author-note"))
> I'm making it a point for myself to write double as much this year. In that spirit, I
> figured I wanted to keep track of other little goals and things as the year goes on, so
> this is the first of those reflections. I also think it's important to call out that I'm
> under no illusions about "productivity hacks," "personal development strategies," or any
> of that bullshit. This is just my own way of looking at where I was and what I was doing
> at a specific moment in my life.

# Stuff I Did
## Ran
* I ran a total of 73.5km, averaging 18.4km weekly.
* This was a total time of 6:29:20 on feet, with my average pace sitting somewhere around a
5:15-5:20 per kilometer, though this obviously varies based on how long the run was.
* Correcting for Garmin's GPS being shitty, I did about 150m total of elevation for the
entire month (most of which was one bridge). Lol. The curse of living in a country where
half of it was reclaimed from the sea.

## Played
This was the first month in many, many months where I had free time to play games multiple
days in a row (largely because I am wilfully unemployed for a bit but we'll ignore that
part). So I got through a bunch of huge stuff I'd had on the backlog, most of it picked up
during the Steam winter sale:
* [*Clair Obscur: Expidition 33*](https://store.steampowered.com/app/1903340/Clair_Obscur_Expedition_33/), which I
wrote my first [rambling review](rr-clair-obscur) about.
* [*Indiana Jones and the Great Circle*](https://store.steampowered.com/app/2677660/Indiana_Jones_and_the_Great_Circle/),
which I also wrote a [rambling review of](rr-indiana-jones-great-circle).
* [*Absolum*](https://store.steampowered.com/app/1904480/Absolum/), a neat little
beat-em-up roguelite. I've only cleared one full run and plan to keep playing it, but it
has been a perfect Steam Deck game.
* [*Unpacking*](https://store.steampowered.com/app/1135690/Unpacking/), a cute little
indie game that shows up on lots of recommendation lists. It was nice and relaxing with my
coffee for a few mornings, but nothing super special, though I can appreciate how it tells
its story.
* [*DOOM: The Dark Ages*](https://store.steampowered.com/app/3017860/DOOM_The_Dark_Ages/),
which has you fight a giant cyborg Cthulu elder god in a mech suit because it is a good
video game. Rambling review in the works on this one!

## Read
I finished 4 books in January:
* [*A Wizard of Earthsea*](https://hardcover.app/books/a-wizard-of-earthsea), which I
enjoyed, though it is definitely a very classic-style fantasy. I have the first 4 books
in one volume, so I'm looking forward to returning to the series soon.
* [*Red Rising*](https://hardcover.app/books/red-rising), which I've seen recommended
everywhere. It was... a mixed bag. Definitely a page turner, but something about it just
felt off to me. Maybe the main character being a bit of a [Gary
Stu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Sue), maybe something with how uncomfortably the
story seems to delight in brutality, not sure. I'll probably finish the original trilogy
in the coming months, but I'm on the fence.
* [*Empire of AI*](https://hardcover.app/books/empire-of-ai), which was fantastic even
though it obviously confirmed most of my own biases against OpenAI. Still highly
recommended reading as it is extensively researched.
* I finally finished [*Designing Data Intensive Applications*](https://hardcover.app/books/designing-data-intensive-applications),
which I bought months ago and have been slowly making my way through. Also excellent
reading, though it is too technical to be my bedtime reading and that's my excuse for why
it took so long to finish.

I also read a ton of other stuff, largely articles from across the internet, probably more
than I should have (but it is at least better than the amount of YouTube I was previously
slamming, and I've all but cut out short-form content). Lots of it was very good, but
there are two things I want to particularly call out:
* [*The year of technoligarchy*](https://www.citationneeded.news/the-year-of-technoligarchy/)
by Molly White is pretty sobering. Citation Needed is an excellent publication to
subscribe to no matter what, but I found this piece especially good. A small quote from
the end that at least brings a glimmer of hope:

> And technoligarchs’ consolidation of power makes them visible architects of these
> systems. When the economy they’ve hollowed out seizes up, when the markets they’ve
> destabilized implode, when the legitimacy of the institutions they’ve captured
> evaporates, and when everyday people suffer, their names are on all of it.
>
> We’re not all gonna make it. But neither, necessarily, are they.

* I've also been making my way through *Harry Potter en de Steen der Wijzen*, the Dutch
translation of the first Harry Potter book. At this point I can get through a single
chapter in half an hour or so without having to really look up most of the vocabulary,
which feels great. I'm expecting the first 3 books to be fairly handleable with my current
level in the language (~B1), which hopefully gives me enough buffer time to keep
practicing above and beyond.

