* I also listened to a lot of [Psychedelic Porn Crumpets](https://psychedelicporncrumpets.bandcamp.com/),
but largely on my phone which unfortunately doesn't get pulled into the stats above. They
are my home workout band of choice right now.

## Built
* One thing I really wanted to make sure I did this month was submit a contribution to a
piece of OSS software that I use frequently. In the spirit of trying to focus on `zig`
this year, I chose to make [this PR to `zine`](https://github.com/kristoff-it/zine/pull/194),
which is the site generator I use for this very site, so felt apt.
* I also converted all my home services to [quadlets](https://docs.podman.io/en/latest/markdown/podman-systemd.unit.5.html),
something I'd been meaning to do for a while. As with every time I go to touch my home
server infrastructure (in other words, ssh into the laptop in the corner), it was
significantly eaiser than I had expected. This is largely because I intentionally expose
nothing outside my local network so I get to skip a whole class of security stuff, but
even still it feels much more robust now.
* I also made some small [eurydice](https://codeberg.org/garrettm/eurydice) improvements
for the stats for this writeup, as mentioned above.
* As with every month, I tweaked my dotfiles endlessly. Highlights include a custom timer
script that I've been getting great use out of, some cleanup of my nvim plugins, and in
general a trend of simplification that I'm very happy with.
* I started working on a small Discord bot which is breaking my "zig only" rule and using
python because the library support for the discord API is so much better (and writing my
own wrapper is tedium I don't want to undertake). Sue me. But I hope to finish it early in
February so my friends and I can use it.

# Stuff I Didn't Do
Of course, I had plenty of other things on my goal/to-do/whatever list, lots of them
personal enough that I don't want to list them on my blog. I did do a lot of them, but
there was of course some stuff that sadly I missed out on, but that's fine. I never expect
to have a "perfect" month, this is just a good time to reflect on if I care about any of
it:
* I intended to run 40km a week in January. I only managed to do this for one week, sadly.
Though I did run every week, there's been an extreme cold snap here in the Netherlands
(and a weirdly high amount of snow), so it's been much harder to drag myself out for
anything more than a quick loop once or twice a week.
* I aimed to eat only vegetarian this month. I broke this rule precisely once, when I was
extremely tired after a very busy day and could not resist buying a burrito with ground
beef. Still, I am very pleasantly surprised with how easy it was otherwise.
* I made a concerted effort to not use any screens during any of my main meals, in an
attempt to try and be a bit more present with my cooking (and also to cook more). This was
a mixed bag, some days it went without a hitch and others I did sadly end up eating while
reading or watching something. I want to keep working on it though.

# Stuff I'm Gonna Do In February
Which brings us to the shortest month! A lot of my goals from January are going to roll
right over into February, but the shortlist of things that I really want to aim for is:
* Run 40km a week, for real this time. It's not a question of fitness or motivation, I
just have to drag my ass out into the cold.
* I have an idea for a  short story that's been kicking around in my head for a few months
now, and I want to finally actually sit down and write a draft of it. Maybe that'll appear
here on my site, maybe I'll only send it to some close friends and family, or maybe it'll
never see any other eyeballs. Who knows. None of those are possible if I don't write it
first.
* Similarly, I've had an application I've wanted to build for myself for long time that I
just haven't written the actual first line of code for. I want to build a usable prototype
for myself in February, especially because the sooner I get something working the more
willing I'll be to keep hacking on it.
* Lastly, I want to start branching out with my cooking. I consider myself a fairly decent
home cook already, but I tend to make the same few things over and over when left to my
own devices (Shakshuka, anyone?). This month I'm aiming to make one completely new thing
every week, by just rolling a 200-sided die for a random country and making something from
there (if I can find the ingredients).

That's it for now, hi Mom!
